1
00:00:01,138 --> 00:00:02,503
On day 211,

2
00:00:02,503 --> 00:00:05,704
nobody in the bunker has slept.

3
00:00:05,704 --> 00:00:07,691
The radio sits on the bench

4
00:00:07,691 --> 00:00:10,035
between the three of you, hissing.

5
00:00:10,035 --> 00:00:12,229
You have taken turns holding

6
00:00:12,229 --> 00:00:14,192
it all night, as if holding it

7
00:00:14,192 --> 00:00:16,641
harder might bring her voice back.

8
00:00:16,641 --> 00:00:17,432
It didn't.

9
00:00:17,432 --> 00:00:19,405
The last thing it said was

10
00:00:19,405 --> 00:00:20,925
the first sound of a name.

11
00:00:20,925 --> 00:00:22,351
Your name is Patch.

12
00:00:22,724 --> 00:00:23,871
Two hundred and ten days ago,

13
00:00:23,871 --> 00:00:26,083
you were waiting for a bus.

14
00:00:26,083 --> 00:00:28,438
Since then, you have lived under a

15
00:00:28,438 --> 00:00:30,990
bus depot with Nora, who used to clean it,

16
00:00:30,990 --> 00:00:33,863
and Wes, who used to live on a roof.

17
00:00:33,863 --> 00:00:35,835
Twenty days ago, the woman from the

18
00:00:35,835 --> 00:00:37,849
truck drove the last bus out of your yard

19
00:00:37,849 --> 00:00:40,632
with the whole horde following her horn.

20
00:00:40,632 --> 00:00:42,321
Your yard is empty now.

21
00:00:42,695 --> 00:00:44,102
Your street is empty.

22
00:00:44,102 --> 00:00:47,246
And she is sitting in that bus on the motorway,

23
00:00:47,246 --> 00:00:48,102
out of fuel,

24
00:00:48,102 --> 00:00:51,129
with every one of them standing around her.

25
00:00:51,129 --> 00:00:51,782
At dawn,

26
00:00:51,782 --> 00:00:54,837
Nora puts the radio down and stands up.

27
00:00:54,837 --> 00:00:57,627
"We're going to get her," she says.

28
00:00:57,627 --> 00:00:59,298
You think of the rule.

29
00:00:59,298 --> 00:01:01,850
If they come toward the gate,

30
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:02,688
you close it.

31
00:01:02,688 --> 00:01:04,725
Even if somebody is outside.

32
00:01:05,083 --> 00:01:06,400
You have kept that

33
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:07,716
rule for two hundred days.

34
00:01:07,716 --> 00:01:10,191
"The rule is about the gate," Nora says,

35
00:01:10,191 --> 00:01:12,298
as if she heard you think it.

36
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:15,382
"It doesn't say anything about a truck."

37
00:01:15,382 --> 00:01:17,191
Nobody asks who we means.

38
00:01:17,191 --> 00:01:20,126
Wes is already looking at the jump leads.

39
00:01:20,483 --> 00:01:22,956
You are already thinking about the truck.

40
00:01:22,956 --> 00:01:25,199
"It's got half a tank," says Wes.

41
00:01:25,199 --> 00:01:27,356
"And no battery to speak of."

42
00:01:27,356 --> 00:01:29,964
You remember a long ramp at night,

43
00:01:29,964 --> 00:01:32,751
and an engine switched off at the top of it.

44
00:01:32,751 --> 00:01:33,649
She taught you how to

45
00:01:33,649 --> 00:01:35,009
stop an engine on a hill.

46
00:01:35,559 --> 00:01:36,769
"Then we'll need a hill

47
00:01:36,769 --> 00:01:38,280
to start one," you say.

48
00:01:39,167 --> 00:01:41,085
Days 212 and 213

49
00:01:41,085 --> 00:01:42,978
are for the truck.

50
00:01:42,978 --> 00:01:44,773
It sits three streets away,

51
00:01:44,773 --> 00:01:47,320
where it died on day 141,

52
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:51,174
white with frost, its cab door hanging open.

53
00:01:51,174 --> 00:01:51,696
Up close,

54
00:01:51,696 --> 00:01:54,169
it looks smaller than you remember.

55
00:01:54,169 --> 00:01:55,058
Most things do,

56
00:01:55,058 --> 00:01:57,664
once you have stopped being afraid of them.

57
00:01:57,664 --> 00:02:00,587
On the radio, you ask the hill for help.

58
00:02:00,587 --> 00:02:03,099
By midday, a line of people with

59
00:02:03,099 --> 00:02:05,379
blue bandages walks down the hill road.

60
00:02:05,379 --> 00:02:06,403
A dozen of them.

61
00:02:06,403 --> 00:02:08,442
They have never pushed a truck before.

62
00:02:08,442 --> 00:02:09,434
Neither have you.

63
00:02:09,434 --> 00:02:11,449
The street where the truck stands

64
00:02:11,449 --> 00:02:13,863
slopes gently down toward the lower town.

65
00:02:13,863 --> 00:02:15,926
You climb into the driver's seat.

66
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:18,432
You have not driven anything since

67
00:02:18,432 --> 00:02:21,055
before day one, and never anything this big.

68
00:02:21,055 --> 00:02:23,141
Wes leans in through the window and

69
00:02:23,141 --> 00:02:26,384
explains the clutch to you in a whisper,

70
00:02:26,384 --> 00:02:28,734
three times, with hand movements.

71
00:02:28,734 --> 00:02:31,106
On day 212, the first try goes wrong.

72
00:02:31,106 --> 00:02:33,844
The truck rolls halfway down the slope,

73
00:02:33,844 --> 00:02:35,463
you let the clutch out too early,

74
00:02:35,463 --> 00:02:37,435
and it stops dead with a jolt

75
00:02:37,435 --> 00:02:39,369
that bounces your head off the roof.

76
00:02:39,369 --> 00:02:41,194
A dozen people with blue bandages

77
00:02:41,194 --> 00:02:43,031
have to push it back up the hill.

78
00:02:43,031 --> 00:02:44,573
None of them complain.

79
00:02:45,028 --> 00:02:46,399
One of them pats the truck,

80
00:02:46,399 --> 00:02:49,176
as if it is a horse that has had a fright.

81
00:02:49,176 --> 00:02:50,965
On the morning of day 213,

82
00:02:50,965 --> 00:02:52,751
everybody pushes.

83
00:02:52,751 --> 00:02:54,013
The truck creaks.

84
00:02:54,013 --> 00:02:54,855
Then rolls.

85
00:02:54,855 --> 00:02:56,685
Then rolls faster,

86
00:02:56,685 --> 00:02:58,480
the tyres crunching on frost,

87
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:01,162
the hill people falling behind, waving.

88
00:03:01,162 --> 00:03:02,781
At the bottom of the slope,

89
00:03:02,781 --> 00:03:05,773
you let the clutch out the way Wes showed you.

90
00:03:05,773 --> 00:03:07,431
The engine coughs.

91
00:03:07,431 --> 00:03:09,071
Coughs again.

92
00:03:09,071 --> 00:03:10,125
Catches.

93
00:03:10,125 --> 00:03:11,941
For two hundred days,

94
00:03:11,941 --> 00:03:14,467
an engine starting has meant every dead

95
00:03:14,467 --> 00:03:16,830
head in town turning toward you.

96
00:03:16,830 --> 00:03:19,754
This time, the only heads that turn are a

97
00:03:19,754 --> 00:03:22,630
dozen living ones, at the top of the street,

98
00:03:22,630 --> 00:03:25,292
cheering without a sound, out of habit.

99
00:03:25,764 --> 00:03:27,010
On day 214,

100
00:03:27,010 --> 00:03:30,107
Nora walks out of the gate.

101
00:03:30,107 --> 00:03:31,491
Not into the yard.

102
00:03:31,491 --> 00:03:31,855
Out.

103
00:03:31,855 --> 00:03:33,394
Into the street.

104
00:03:33,847 --> 00:03:35,530
She has not been outside the depot

105
00:03:35,530 --> 00:03:37,679
walls since the morning it all started.

106
00:03:37,679 --> 00:03:39,506
She stops on the pavement,

107
00:03:39,506 --> 00:03:41,758
looks up and down the empty street,

108
00:03:41,758 --> 00:03:43,878
and holds the flashlight a little tighter.

109
00:03:44,524 --> 00:03:46,899
"It's bigger out here," she says.

110
00:03:46,899 --> 00:03:48,824
She turns and looks back at

111
00:03:48,824 --> 00:03:50,404
the depot from the outside.

112
00:03:50,404 --> 00:03:51,268
The wall.

113
00:03:51,577 --> 00:03:52,246
The gate.

114
00:03:52,246 --> 00:03:53,987
The sign over the street,

115
00:03:53,987 --> 00:03:56,997
its yellow paint run by the rain.

116
00:03:56,997 --> 00:03:58,386
THREE ALIVE.

117
00:03:58,936 --> 00:04:01,471
Only one of them is staying behind it.

118
00:04:01,471 --> 00:04:03,573
"Everything is," says Wes,

119
00:04:03,573 --> 00:04:04,986
who is staying behind.

120
00:04:04,986 --> 00:04:07,352
Somebody has to keep the depot,

121
00:04:07,352 --> 00:04:08,776
and he says he has always wanted

122
00:04:08,776 --> 00:04:10,586
to be the one waiting at the gate.

123
00:04:10,959 --> 00:04:12,054
He has the radio,

124
00:04:12,054 --> 00:04:15,090
the rope on the bolt and the canopy ladder.

125
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:17,034
"If anything follows you

126
00:04:17,034 --> 00:04:19,534
home," he says, "I'll still be here."

127
00:04:19,534 --> 00:04:23,004
Nora climbs into the cab beside you.

128
00:04:23,004 --> 00:04:25,370
You put the orange bag between your feet.

129
00:04:25,370 --> 00:04:27,479
And for the first time in two hundred

130
00:04:27,479 --> 00:04:31,697
and fourteen days, the only one left behind

131
00:04:31,697 --> 00:04:34,528
the last safe door is a man in a red hat.

132
00:04:35,047 --> 00:04:37,353
You drive down through the lower town,

133
00:04:37,353 --> 00:04:39,701
past the bus stop where a walker

134
00:04:39,701 --> 00:04:42,513
used to wait, past the dark petrol station,

135
00:04:42,513 --> 00:04:45,107
and up the long ramp of the flyover.

136
00:04:45,107 --> 00:04:48,377
At the top, you look down at the town.

137
00:04:48,377 --> 00:04:49,098
The hill.

138
00:04:49,098 --> 00:04:50,437
The tanks.

139
00:04:50,437 --> 00:04:51,908
The depot, very small,

140
00:04:51,908 --> 00:04:54,730
with one tiny figure on the gantry,

141
00:04:55,087 --> 00:04:57,321
flashing a mirror in the morning sun.

142
00:04:57,321 --> 00:04:58,738
Then the motorway.

143
00:04:59,336 --> 00:05:01,657
It is long and flat and grey.

144
00:05:01,657 --> 00:05:04,433
Abandoned cars stand in the lanes

145
00:05:04,433 --> 00:05:06,788
like toys somebody stopped playing with.

146
00:05:07,434 --> 00:05:10,146
Nora stares at all of it through the windshield,

147
00:05:10,146 --> 00:05:11,042
as if she is

148
00:05:11,042 --> 00:05:13,274
reading a book she always meant to read.

149
00:05:13,274 --> 00:05:15,065
Every few kilometers,

150
00:05:15,065 --> 00:05:18,334
you see a few stragglers wandering on the hard shoulder,

151
00:05:18,334 --> 00:05:19,980
walking toward the city.

152
00:05:19,980 --> 00:05:21,033
You don't stop.

153
00:05:21,033 --> 00:05:22,989
You don't use the horn.

154
00:05:22,989 --> 00:05:24,806
Their heads turn slowly to follow

155
00:05:24,806 --> 00:05:26,602
the truck as it passes,

156
00:05:26,602 --> 00:05:28,554
and Nora counts them under her breath,

157
00:05:28,554 --> 00:05:30,825
the way she used to count walkers gone.

158
00:05:30,825 --> 00:05:34,097
By afternoon, you pass under a bridge.

159
00:05:34,614 --> 00:05:36,081
Beyond it, far ahead,

160
00:05:36,081 --> 00:05:39,601
the first houses of the city show against the sky.

161
00:05:39,601 --> 00:05:40,995
And in front of them,

162
00:05:40,995 --> 00:05:42,499
across every lane,

163
00:05:42,953 --> 00:05:45,550
there is something that looks like a dark lake.

164
00:05:45,550 --> 00:05:46,770
It isn't a lake.

165
00:05:47,272 --> 00:05:48,357
It is the river.

166
00:05:48,357 --> 00:05:49,869
Thousands of them,

167
00:05:50,195 --> 00:05:52,972
standing shoulder to shoulder across the whole

168
00:05:52,972 --> 00:05:54,700
width of the motorway,

169
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:57,415
packed so tight that they look like one thing.

170
00:05:58,030 --> 00:05:59,829
All of them facing the middle.

171
00:05:59,829 --> 00:06:00,932
And in the middle,

172
00:06:00,932 --> 00:06:04,898
like a stone in a stream, a beige coach.

173
00:06:05,497 --> 00:06:07,166
On the roof of the coach,

174
00:06:07,166 --> 00:06:10,379
beside the open hatch, a small figure sits

175
00:06:10,379 --> 00:06:13,005
cross-legged with a grey scarf around her neck.

176
00:06:13,005 --> 00:06:15,014
Her goggles catch the light.

177
00:06:15,014 --> 00:06:17,340
"Two doors," Nora whispers.

178
00:06:17,714 --> 00:06:19,441
"She went out the second one."

179
00:06:19,441 --> 00:06:20,870
You stop the truck well

180
00:06:20,870 --> 00:06:23,046
back and turn off the engine.

181
00:06:23,046 --> 00:06:24,947
On the radio, you try her channel.

182
00:06:24,947 --> 00:06:25,736
Nothing.

183
00:06:26,046 --> 00:06:27,176
Her battery is gone.

184
00:06:27,176 --> 00:06:29,114
But as you watch, the figure on the

185
00:06:29,114 --> 00:06:32,268
coach roof slowly raises one arm and waves.

186
00:06:32,268 --> 00:06:33,303
Once.

187
00:06:33,303 --> 00:06:34,538
Still here.

188
00:06:34,538 --> 00:06:37,694
On day 215, you make your plan.

189
00:06:37,694 --> 00:06:39,106
The motorway has two sides,

190
00:06:39,106 --> 00:06:41,562
with a long metal barrier down the middle.

191
00:06:41,562 --> 00:06:43,431
The coach is on the city side.

192
00:06:43,431 --> 00:06:45,835
The other side is almost empty.

193
00:06:45,835 --> 00:06:47,178
The dead do not climb.

194
00:06:47,178 --> 00:06:48,748
A waist-high barrier is

195
00:06:48,748 --> 00:06:50,394
as good as a wall to them.

196
00:06:50,394 --> 00:06:52,067
So at first light,

197
00:06:52,067 --> 00:06:53,990
you cross over to the empty side and

198
00:06:53,990 --> 00:06:55,902
drive slowly toward the coach.

199
00:06:56,276 --> 00:06:57,687
Nora holds the flashlight

200
00:06:57,687 --> 00:06:59,152
in her lap like a promise.

201
00:06:59,152 --> 00:07:01,726
When you are level with the edge of the river,

202
00:07:01,726 --> 00:07:03,274
you put your hand on the horn.

203
00:07:03,632 --> 00:07:04,777
One short blast.

204
00:07:04,777 --> 00:07:07,368
Every head on the motorway turns.

205
00:07:07,743 --> 00:07:08,985
It is like watching a

206
00:07:08,985 --> 00:07:10,847
field of wheat turn in the wind.

207
00:07:10,847 --> 00:07:12,915
Thousands of faces swing away

208
00:07:12,915 --> 00:07:14,730
from the coach and toward the truck.

209
00:07:14,730 --> 00:07:16,485
Then they start to move.

210
00:07:16,485 --> 00:07:18,208
They come up against the barrier

211
00:07:18,208 --> 00:07:21,055
and press there, and as you roll forward,

212
00:07:21,055 --> 00:07:23,759
very slowly, they follow you along it,

213
00:07:23,759 --> 00:07:25,531
a whole motorway of the dead

214
00:07:25,531 --> 00:07:28,081
walking beside a truck, toward the city.

215
00:07:28,454 --> 00:07:29,510
You keep going.

216
00:07:29,510 --> 00:07:30,632
One short blast.

217
00:07:30,632 --> 00:07:31,526
Another.

218
00:07:31,979 --> 00:07:32,666
Behind you,

219
00:07:32,666 --> 00:07:34,978
the river pulls away from the coach.

220
00:07:34,978 --> 00:07:37,613
Slowly, the coach comes out of the crowd,

221
00:07:37,613 --> 00:07:39,651
like a rock when the tide goes out.

222
00:07:39,992 --> 00:07:41,309
The barrier creaks.

223
00:07:41,309 --> 00:07:42,663
Then it groans.

224
00:07:43,261 --> 00:07:44,686
A whole section of it leans

225
00:07:44,686 --> 00:07:46,569
toward you under the weight of a few

226
00:07:46,569 --> 00:07:48,936
thousand people pressing against it.

227
00:07:48,936 --> 00:07:50,808
You don't speed up.

228
00:07:50,808 --> 00:07:52,989
Speeding up is how you lose them.

229
00:07:52,989 --> 00:07:54,962
You just keep rolling,

230
00:07:54,962 --> 00:07:57,266
one short blast at every lamppost,

231
00:07:57,266 --> 00:08:00,561
and you try not to look at the barrier at all.

232
00:08:00,561 --> 00:08:02,869
Two kilometers on, there is a junction.

233
00:08:03,307 --> 00:08:05,514
A long slip road curves up onto

234
00:08:05,514 --> 00:08:08,083
a bridge and down the far side.

235
00:08:08,083 --> 00:08:09,796
You remember a woman holding a

236
00:08:09,796 --> 00:08:12,863
steering wheel with two fingers, like a teacup.

237
00:08:12,863 --> 00:08:14,333
At the top of the bridge,

238
00:08:14,333 --> 00:08:16,879
you stop and lean on the horn.

239
00:08:16,879 --> 00:08:17,637
Not short.

240
00:08:17,637 --> 00:08:18,051
Long.

241
00:08:18,051 --> 00:08:20,789
It rolls out over the motorway and the

242
00:08:20,789 --> 00:08:22,735
fields and the first houses of the city.

243
00:08:22,735 --> 00:08:24,079
Then you turn the key,

244
00:08:24,079 --> 00:08:25,553
and the engine dies.

245
00:08:25,553 --> 00:08:27,463
The truck begins to roll.

246
00:08:27,463 --> 00:08:29,920
Down the far side of the slip road,

247
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:33,756
silent, no lights, faster and faster.

248
00:08:33,756 --> 00:08:36,070
Nora grips the dashboard.

249
00:08:36,070 --> 00:08:37,500
You hold the wheel with two

250
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:39,844
fingers and try very hard to look like

251
00:08:39,844 --> 00:08:41,910
someone who has done this before.

252
00:08:41,910 --> 00:08:43,318
At the bottom,

253
00:08:43,318 --> 00:08:45,352
you coast on along an empty road,

254
00:08:45,352 --> 00:08:48,430
slow down, and stop behind a row of trees.

255
00:08:48,430 --> 00:08:49,662
Nora lets go of the

256
00:08:49,662 --> 00:08:51,758
dashboard one finger at a time.

257
00:08:51,758 --> 00:08:52,938
And you wait.

258
00:08:53,425 --> 00:08:54,179
Two hours.

259
00:08:54,179 --> 00:08:57,162
By then, a kilometer is a wall.

260
00:08:57,601 --> 00:08:58,645
When you look back,

261
00:08:58,645 --> 00:09:00,837
the bridge at the top of the slip road is

262
00:09:00,837 --> 00:09:03,116
black from one railing to the other.

263
00:09:03,116 --> 00:09:05,008
The river is standing exactly where the

264
00:09:05,008 --> 00:09:07,446
last sound was, facing nowhere,

265
00:09:07,446 --> 00:09:10,898
waiting for a horn that is never going to blast again.

266
00:09:10,898 --> 00:09:12,842
You drive back to the coach on

267
00:09:12,842 --> 00:09:14,355
the empty side of the motorway

268
00:09:14,355 --> 00:09:15,869
with your heart in your mouth.

269
00:09:15,869 --> 00:09:18,571
It sits alone in the middle of its lanes.

270
00:09:18,571 --> 00:09:19,796
Almost alone.

271
00:09:20,233 --> 00:09:22,037
A handful of stragglers still

272
00:09:22,037 --> 00:09:24,170
stand around it, the slow ones and

273
00:09:24,170 --> 00:09:25,289
the stubborn ones,

274
00:09:25,289 --> 00:09:28,812
the ones who never follow anything the first time.

275
00:09:28,812 --> 00:09:31,229
On the coach roof, the woman stands up.

276
00:09:31,229 --> 00:09:33,998
She reaches into the folds of her scarf,

277
00:09:33,998 --> 00:09:35,264
pulls out a small travel

278
00:09:35,264 --> 00:09:37,481
alarm clock with two bells on top,

279
00:09:37,481 --> 00:09:40,511
winds it, and throws it as far as she can,

280
00:09:40,511 --> 00:09:42,478
over the hard shoulder and into

281
00:09:42,478 --> 00:09:45,032
the frozen field beside the motorway.

282
00:09:45,032 --> 00:09:48,298
A few seconds later, it starts to ring.

283
00:09:48,298 --> 00:09:50,080
A tiny, furious sound.

284
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:52,397
The stragglers turn toward it,

285
00:09:52,397 --> 00:09:53,190
one by one,

286
00:09:53,190 --> 00:09:56,130
and wander off into the field after a clock.

287
00:09:56,130 --> 00:09:59,209
They do not leave when the noise stops.

288
00:09:59,209 --> 00:10:00,456
They never do.

289
00:10:00,456 --> 00:10:02,534
Every bus needs a clock.

290
00:10:02,534 --> 00:10:04,673
She climbs down through the hatch,

291
00:10:04,673 --> 00:10:06,462
out through the folding door,

292
00:10:06,773 --> 00:10:11,092
across the lanes and over the barrier, without hurrying once.

293
00:10:11,092 --> 00:10:12,979
She opens the door of the truck,

294
00:10:12,979 --> 00:10:15,283
climbs into the cab beside Nora,

295
00:10:15,283 --> 00:10:16,663
and sits there for a moment,

296
00:10:16,663 --> 00:10:19,978
with her goggles pushed up, looking straight ahead.

297
00:10:19,978 --> 00:10:21,602
Nora doesn't wait.

298
00:10:21,602 --> 00:10:23,957
"Say it again," she says.

299
00:10:23,957 --> 00:10:26,903
The woman looks at her for a long time.

300
00:10:27,310 --> 00:10:28,359
Then she smiles,

301
00:10:28,359 --> 00:10:30,757
which you have never seen her do.

302
00:10:31,484 --> 00:10:32,852
"Vera," she says.

303
00:10:32,852 --> 00:10:35,332
Nora nods slowly, as if she is

304
00:10:35,332 --> 00:10:37,255
writing it down somewhere inside.

305
00:10:37,255 --> 00:10:39,930
Then she knocks on Vera's shoulder.

306
00:10:39,930 --> 00:10:40,563
Three times.

307
00:10:40,563 --> 00:10:42,951
Then a fourth time, softer.

308
00:10:42,951 --> 00:10:46,032
"It was a good bus," says Vera,

309
00:10:46,032 --> 00:10:47,457
looking back at the coach

310
00:10:47,457 --> 00:10:49,047
standing empty in its lanes.

311
00:10:49,047 --> 00:10:52,625
"Tell the night driver I said so."

312
00:10:52,625 --> 00:10:56,211
From day 216 to day 218,

313
00:10:56,211 --> 00:10:59,787
you drive home the long way, and nobody is in a hurry.

314
00:11:00,306 --> 00:11:02,274
You stop for the night on the flyover,

315
00:11:02,274 --> 00:11:04,796
at the very top, because from there you

316
00:11:04,796 --> 00:11:07,012
can see anything coming for kilometers.

317
00:11:07,402 --> 00:11:09,196
Vera sleeps sitting up in the cab,

318
00:11:09,196 --> 00:11:11,767
facing the door, out of habit.

319
00:11:11,767 --> 00:11:13,029
But she sleeps.

320
00:11:13,029 --> 00:11:15,495
Nora stays awake looking at the sky.

321
00:11:15,495 --> 00:11:17,031
She has been under a ceiling

322
00:11:17,031 --> 00:11:18,936
for so long that she has

323
00:11:18,936 --> 00:11:21,569
forgotten how many stars there are.

324
00:11:21,569 --> 00:11:23,041
On day 216,

325
00:11:23,041 --> 00:11:24,788
Vera tells you about the coach.

326
00:11:24,788 --> 00:11:26,396
When the fuel ran out,

327
00:11:26,396 --> 00:11:28,632
she climbed onto the roof and stayed there,

328
00:11:28,632 --> 00:11:31,758
because from up there she could see everything coming.

329
00:11:31,758 --> 00:11:32,883
She drank the frost that

330
00:11:32,883 --> 00:11:34,983
formed on the hatch every morning.

331
00:11:34,983 --> 00:11:36,805
She wound the clock every day at dawn,

332
00:11:36,805 --> 00:11:39,856
so she would know she was still here.

333
00:11:39,856 --> 00:11:42,488
On day 217, you ask Vera what

334
00:11:42,488 --> 00:11:44,397
the city looked like up close.

335
00:11:44,397 --> 00:11:46,491
"Empty streets," she says.

336
00:11:46,491 --> 00:11:47,590
"Shut windows.

337
00:11:47,590 --> 00:11:50,234
And one night, on the edge of it,

338
00:11:50,234 --> 00:11:52,131
one window with a light in it.

339
00:11:52,131 --> 00:11:53,043
Just one."

340
00:11:53,578 --> 00:11:54,317
"Living?"

341
00:11:54,317 --> 00:11:57,298
"Or very tidy dead," she says.

342
00:11:57,656 --> 00:12:00,541
"I wasn't in a position to knock."

343
00:12:00,541 --> 00:12:03,320
On day 218, you drive down off the

344
00:12:03,320 --> 00:12:06,107
flyover into the lower town, and the gantry of

345
00:12:06,107 --> 00:12:08,840
the depot comes into view over the rooftops.

346
00:12:08,840 --> 00:12:11,723
A small light blinks at the end of it,

347
00:12:11,723 --> 00:12:13,321
in broad daylight, twice.

348
00:12:13,321 --> 00:12:14,939
Once for still here.

349
00:12:14,939 --> 00:12:16,981
Once for all of you.

350
00:12:16,981 --> 00:12:19,680
On day 219, you stop the truck in

351
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:22,813
front of your gate and turn the engine off.

352
00:12:22,813 --> 00:12:25,941
Nobody inside needs to be told who it is.

353
00:12:25,941 --> 00:12:27,864
But Nora gets out anyway,

354
00:12:27,864 --> 00:12:30,740
walks up to the gate and knocks.

355
00:12:30,740 --> 00:12:31,336
Three times.

356
00:12:31,336 --> 00:12:33,261
Then a fourth time, softer.

357
00:12:33,261 --> 00:12:34,441
The bolt lifts.

358
00:12:34,441 --> 00:12:36,182
The gate slides open

359
00:12:36,182 --> 00:12:38,066
with a polite little whisper.

360
00:12:38,066 --> 00:12:39,710
And there is Wes,

361
00:12:39,710 --> 00:12:43,468
in his red hat, with his arms out, as if he has

362
00:12:43,468 --> 00:12:45,286
been standing there for five days.

363
00:12:45,286 --> 00:12:46,552
He probably has.

364
00:12:47,150 --> 00:12:49,016
"Welcome to the depot," he says.

365
00:12:49,016 --> 00:12:51,301
"Management apologizes for the delay."

366
00:12:51,301 --> 00:12:53,642
Vera walks in through the gate.

367
00:12:53,642 --> 00:12:55,333
For a while, she just stands in the

368
00:12:55,333 --> 00:12:58,070
middle of the empty yard and turns around slowly,

369
00:12:58,070 --> 00:12:59,604
looking at the walls,

370
00:12:59,604 --> 00:13:03,438
the canopy, the bay door, the gantry.

371
00:13:03,438 --> 00:13:05,682
A place with one way out.

372
00:13:05,682 --> 00:13:07,477
Her eyes stop on the dark

373
00:13:07,477 --> 00:13:09,411
rectangle on the concrete,

374
00:13:09,411 --> 00:13:12,365
where the coach stood for a hundred and ninety days.

375
00:13:13,221 --> 00:13:14,542
"Wes," she says.

376
00:13:14,542 --> 00:13:16,251
"My name's Vera."

377
00:13:16,899 --> 00:13:18,287
Wes takes off his hat,

378
00:13:18,287 --> 00:13:19,977
the way he did once before,

379
00:13:19,977 --> 00:13:23,161
on day 77, and puts it on his chest.

380
00:13:23,161 --> 00:13:25,134
"I know," he says.

381
00:13:25,134 --> 00:13:27,436
"Nora told me on the radio.

382
00:13:27,436 --> 00:13:28,504
Four times."

383
00:13:29,040 --> 00:13:32,894
From day 220 to day 225,

384
00:13:32,894 --> 00:13:34,629
the depot changes its sign.

385
00:13:34,629 --> 00:13:36,116
Nora fetches the tin of

386
00:13:36,116 --> 00:13:37,641
white paint from the rack.

387
00:13:37,641 --> 00:13:40,264
She climbs to the far end of the gantry,

388
00:13:40,264 --> 00:13:42,072
leans out over the street,

389
00:13:42,072 --> 00:13:45,605
crosses out THREE, and paints a big, careful FOUR.

390
00:13:45,605 --> 00:13:47,040
She takes her time.

391
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:48,603
When she comes back,

392
00:13:48,603 --> 00:13:50,726
she has white paint on her purple

393
00:13:50,726 --> 00:13:53,643
band and does not care at all.

394
00:13:53,643 --> 00:13:55,448
FOUR ALIVE.

395
00:13:55,919 --> 00:13:58,529
Wes goes to the chart on the bunker wall.

396
00:13:58,529 --> 00:13:59,275
People met.

397
00:13:59,275 --> 00:14:00,708
He adds a mark,

398
00:14:00,708 --> 00:14:02,343
then stops, and looks at Vera.

399
00:14:02,343 --> 00:14:03,733
"Do you count twice?"

400
00:14:04,348 --> 00:14:06,138
"I count once," says Vera.

401
00:14:06,138 --> 00:14:07,942
"Like everybody else."

402
00:14:08,477 --> 00:14:10,173
Nora takes the pencil from him.

403
00:14:10,173 --> 00:14:11,709
She rubs out the fourth column,

404
00:14:11,709 --> 00:14:14,092
the one that counted down to the river,

405
00:14:14,092 --> 00:14:16,896
the only column you ever kept that went backward.

406
00:14:16,896 --> 00:14:19,310
And at the top, she writes a new one.

407
00:14:19,310 --> 00:14:20,484
Days in daylight.

408
00:14:20,484 --> 00:14:23,292
It already has twenty-nine marks.

409
00:14:23,292 --> 00:14:27,395
From day 226 to day 232,

410
00:14:27,395 --> 00:14:30,185
the depot opens its doors,

411
00:14:30,185 --> 00:14:31,220
one at a time.

412
00:14:31,220 --> 00:14:33,337
You take the desk and the

413
00:14:33,337 --> 00:14:34,831
trolley away from the bay door.

414
00:14:34,831 --> 00:14:35,858
The suitcases go back

415
00:14:35,858 --> 00:14:37,779
where they came from, mostly.

416
00:14:37,779 --> 00:14:40,982
The bay door swings open onto an empty yard,

417
00:14:40,982 --> 00:14:43,048
and it stays open all afternoon,

418
00:14:43,048 --> 00:14:46,199
for no reason except that it can.

419
00:14:46,199 --> 00:14:49,067
On day 228, Nora walks to the far

420
00:14:49,067 --> 00:14:50,530
end of the depot building,

421
00:14:50,530 --> 00:14:53,124
to a narrow room you have never been in.

422
00:14:53,124 --> 00:14:55,222
A row of tall metal lockers.

423
00:14:55,596 --> 00:14:57,080
She stops at one of them and

424
00:14:57,080 --> 00:14:58,410
opens it with a small key

425
00:14:58,410 --> 00:15:00,317
from the bottom of her teal bag.

426
00:15:00,317 --> 00:15:03,023
Inside, there is a folded cleaning jacket.

427
00:15:03,023 --> 00:15:04,203
A pair of gloves.

428
00:15:04,203 --> 00:15:07,057
And taped to the inside of the door,

429
00:15:07,057 --> 00:15:08,441
a postcard of a beach,

430
00:15:08,441 --> 00:15:10,698
bright blue sea and yellow sand.

431
00:15:10,698 --> 00:15:13,284
"I always meant to go," she says.

432
00:15:13,659 --> 00:15:15,678
Wes leans in to look at it.

433
00:15:15,678 --> 00:15:17,780
"That's the one," he says.

434
00:15:18,219 --> 00:15:19,602
"That's the beach.

435
00:15:19,602 --> 00:15:20,524
I promised."

436
00:15:20,524 --> 00:15:23,405
She doesn't take the postcard down.

437
00:15:23,405 --> 00:15:25,712
She just leaves the locker open.

438
00:15:26,344 --> 00:15:28,650
On day 230, you drive the truck to

439
00:15:28,650 --> 00:15:31,279
the hill with Vera, and bring back sacks

440
00:15:31,279 --> 00:15:33,890
of potatoes and apples and jars of beans,

441
00:15:33,890 --> 00:15:35,744
and you cook them on a fire in the

442
00:15:35,744 --> 00:15:39,556
middle of the yard, and nobody whispers.

443
00:15:39,556 --> 00:15:43,584
From day 233 to day 239,

444
00:15:43,584 --> 00:15:45,861
the four of you

445
00:15:45,861 --> 00:15:47,446
learn to live in daylight.

446
00:15:47,446 --> 00:15:48,326
Every morning,

447
00:15:48,326 --> 00:15:52,232
the truck goes out, one short blast at every corner,

448
00:15:52,232 --> 00:15:54,193
and leads whatever wandered

449
00:15:54,193 --> 00:15:56,721
into town overnight back out of it.

450
00:15:57,192 --> 00:15:59,018
Some mornings Vera drives.

451
00:15:59,018 --> 00:16:00,753
Some mornings, you do.

452
00:16:01,208 --> 00:16:02,617
The hill sends a boy with a

453
00:16:02,617 --> 00:16:04,743
blue bandage to learn the code.

454
00:16:04,743 --> 00:16:06,977
Wes oils the gate every three days,

455
00:16:06,977 --> 00:16:09,363
although it doesn't need it.

456
00:16:09,363 --> 00:16:12,454
Nora sits in the yard in the sun every afternoon,

457
00:16:12,454 --> 00:16:14,459
against the same canopy pillar.

458
00:16:14,459 --> 00:16:16,846
Vera still sleeps sitting up,

459
00:16:16,846 --> 00:16:17,794
facing a door.

460
00:16:17,794 --> 00:16:19,565
But now she leaves it open.

461
00:16:19,565 --> 00:16:21,799
At night, you all go back

462
00:16:21,799 --> 00:16:23,366
down to the bunker anyway.

463
00:16:23,366 --> 00:16:25,322
Up there, the sky is too big.

464
00:16:25,322 --> 00:16:27,173
Down here, there is a bench,

465
00:16:27,173 --> 00:16:29,070
a lantern, a wall chart,

466
00:16:29,070 --> 00:16:31,051
and a ceiling with nothing on it.

467
00:16:31,051 --> 00:16:32,436
You have never slept

468
00:16:32,436 --> 00:16:34,029
so well under anything.

469
00:16:34,029 --> 00:16:36,777
The loudspeaker battery is full again.

470
00:16:36,777 --> 00:16:39,103
Wes checks that the switch says OFF,

471
00:16:39,103 --> 00:16:40,164
then tapes it down

472
00:16:40,164 --> 00:16:42,062
with a strip of tape, very neatly.

473
00:16:42,062 --> 00:16:44,169
Some promises are worth keeping,

474
00:16:44,169 --> 00:16:45,638
even to a loudspeaker.

475
00:16:45,638 --> 00:16:49,714
On day 240, at sunset, the radio crackles.

476
00:16:49,714 --> 00:16:50,515
It isn't Vera.

477
00:16:50,515 --> 00:16:52,329
She is sitting right next to you.

478
00:16:52,329 --> 00:16:53,643
It isn't the hill.

479
00:16:54,097 --> 00:16:56,425
You know every voice up there now.

480
00:16:56,425 --> 00:16:58,368
It is a new voice, a man's,

481
00:16:58,368 --> 00:17:00,878
on the exact channel painted on your sign.

482
00:17:00,878 --> 00:17:01,820
Careful.

483
00:17:01,820 --> 00:17:02,658
Tired.

484
00:17:02,658 --> 00:17:03,616
Polite.

485
00:17:03,616 --> 00:17:04,952
"Depot.

486
00:17:04,952 --> 00:17:05,427
Depot.

487
00:17:05,427 --> 00:17:06,309
Do you read?"

488
00:17:06,309 --> 00:17:09,030
The four of you look at each other.

489
00:17:09,030 --> 00:17:10,672
You pick up the radio.

490
00:17:10,672 --> 00:17:12,028
"This is the depot."

491
00:17:12,028 --> 00:17:13,327
A long pause.

492
00:17:13,942 --> 00:17:16,106
"This is the city," says the voice.

493
00:17:16,106 --> 00:17:18,047
"A month ago, somebody drove a bus

494
00:17:18,047 --> 00:17:19,665
up the motorway with the biggest

495
00:17:19,665 --> 00:17:21,841
horde we have ever seen behind it,

496
00:17:21,841 --> 00:17:23,825
and left it standing on our doorstep.

497
00:17:23,825 --> 00:17:25,692
Then a white truck came along and

498
00:17:25,692 --> 00:17:27,641
parked the whole thing on our bridge.

499
00:17:27,641 --> 00:17:29,035
The bus had your depot's

500
00:17:29,035 --> 00:17:30,670
name painted on the side."

501
00:17:30,670 --> 00:17:32,632
Vera closes her eyes.

502
00:17:32,632 --> 00:17:34,184
Her goggles are in her hand,

503
00:17:34,184 --> 00:17:36,262
and she turns them over and over,

504
00:17:36,262 --> 00:17:39,737
the way Nora held the keys on day 167.

505
00:17:40,592 --> 00:17:41,760
"We'd like to talk to

506
00:17:41,760 --> 00:17:43,618
the driver," says the voice.

507
00:17:44,024 --> 00:17:45,128
Two hundred and forty days.

508
00:17:45,128 --> 00:17:48,188
The same orange bandage.

509
00:17:48,626 --> 00:17:50,740
Nora is holding the flashlight.

510
00:17:50,740 --> 00:17:53,162
You are holding the orange bag.

511
00:17:53,162 --> 00:17:56,078
Tomorrow is day 241.

512
00:17:56,628 --> 00:17:57,819
Your yard is empty,

513
00:17:57,819 --> 00:17:59,190
your gate is your own,

514
00:17:59,190 --> 00:18:02,173
and the sign over the street says FOUR ALIVE.

515
00:18:02,173 --> 00:18:04,281
But the river you sent

516
00:18:04,281 --> 00:18:05,534
away didn't disappear.

517
00:18:05,534 --> 00:18:07,332
It went to somebody else's door.

518
00:18:07,332 --> 00:18:08,957
And now, for the first time,

519
00:18:08,957 --> 00:18:11,638
the living are knocking on yours to ask why.

520
00:18:12,189 --> 00:18:13,986
Would you have gone to the motorway?

521
00:18:13,986 --> 00:18:15,006
Which decision would

522
00:18:15,006 --> 00:18:16,504
you have made differently?

523
00:18:16,504 --> 00:18:18,016
Tell us in the comments,

524
00:18:18,016 --> 00:18:20,551
and subscribe to Last Safe Door,

525
00:18:20,551 --> 00:18:22,825
so you're here for day 241.
